<h2>The anatomy of the board-wide FIR</h2>
<p>The pattern is familiar in commercial practice. A supply contract, distributorship, investment or joint venture fails; payments stop or quality disputes erupt; and the aggrieved party, rather than suing for recovery, registers an FIR under Section 318(4) of the BNS (cheating, formerly Section 420 IPC) and Section 316 of the BNS (criminal breach of trust, formerly Section 406 IPC). The FIR frequently names the company and every individual whose name appears on the master data — managing director, whole-time directors, independent and non-executive directors, occasionally directors who resigned before the transaction. The strategy is transparent: the pressure of potential arrest is expected to produce a settlement that civil process would take years to deliver.</p>

<h2>No general vicarious liability in criminal law</h2>
<p>The legal starting point for every director so named is a simple proposition: criminal law knows no general doctrine of vicarious liability. A company acts through individuals, but an individual is criminally liable only for acts or omissions attributable to him or her personally, done with the requisite mental element — or where a statute in terms makes a person in charge of the company answerable, as some special statutes do. The offences typically invoked in commercial FIRs contain no such deeming provision. For cheating, the prosecution must show deception and dishonest inducement at the inception of the transaction; for criminal breach of trust, entrustment to the accused and dishonest misappropriation by the accused. An FIR that recites the transaction and then simply lists the board has pleaded a designation, not an offence.</p>

<h2>Arrest-necessity discipline and the Section 35(3) notice</h2>
<p>The BNSS builds a layer of discipline between registration of an FIR and arrest. For a broad category of cases, the expectation is that police will proceed by a notice under Section 35(3) BNSS (formerly Section 41A CrPC) requiring the person to appear, rather than by arrest, unless recorded reasons make arrest necessary. For a director, the notice is both an obligation and an opportunity: appearing with documents, answering questions and maintaining a written record of each appearance constructs precisely the history of cooperation that anticipatory bail courts look for. Where there is a real apprehension that appearance will be converted into arrest — because co-accused have been arrested, or the investigating officer has said as much — the application under Section 482 BNSS (formerly Section 438 CrPC) should be moved without waiting for the apprehension to materialise.</p>

<h2>Building the Section 482 BNSS application</h2>
<p>A director's anticipatory bail application in a commercial-fallout FIR is won or lost on specificity. The working checklist:</p>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plead the specific-role gap.</strong> Set out what the FIR actually attributes to the applicant by name — usually nothing beyond the designation — and contrast it with the ingredients of the offences invoked.</li>
    <li><strong>Annex the governance record.</strong> Board minutes, resolutions and the delegation-of-authority matrix showing who was empowered to negotiate, sign and operate the relevant accounts, and that the applicant was not.</li>
    <li><strong>Show the civil skeleton.</strong> The contract, invoices, part payments, ledger confirmations and prior correspondence that frame the dispute as a commercial default rather than a fraud conceived at inception. Part performance and a running account are powerful indicators that dishonest intention did not exist when the transaction began.</li>
    <li><strong>Document cooperation.</strong> Every Section 35(3) notice received and complied with, every document produced, with dates.</li>
    <li><strong>Offer conditions.</strong> A concrete undertaking to join the investigation, produce records, and not leave the country without leave.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>Non-executive and independent directors have an additional layer of material: the statutory architecture of company law itself distinguishes their role from executive management, and attendance records showing absence from the relevant meetings, or appointment after the transaction, translate the abstract point into dates the court can verify.</p>

<h2>What conditions to expect</h2>
<p>Where protection is granted, the conditions in these matters follow a settled pattern, and an applicant should arrive prepared to accept them.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Condition</th><th>What it means in practice</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Joining the investigation</td><td>Appearing before the investigating officer as and when called, within a stipulated notice period, and cooperating with the examination of records.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Non-interference</td><td>No contact with the complainant or witnesses in relation to the case, and no tampering with company records relevant to the transaction.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Travel restraint</td><td>Not leaving the country without prior leave of the court; deposit of the passport is sometimes directed, which matters for directors with genuine business travel and can be addressed by seeking a leave mechanism rather than an absolute bar.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No inducement of settlement by threat</td><td>Courts occasionally record that the protection granted shall not be used to stall legitimate civil or recovery proceedings between the parties.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A condition requiring deposit of the disputed commercial amount is occasionally sought by complainants. The principled position is that bail conditions exist to secure presence and protect the investigation, not to execute an unadjudicated civil claim, and the application should be argued on that footing.</p>

<h2>The longer game</h2>
<p>Anticipatory bail stabilises the situation; it does not end the case. Once protection is in place, the director's team can address the investigation on the merits, pursue clarity on each individual's role in any report filed, and let the civil character of the dispute emerge through the documents. The order granting protection — particularly one that records the absence of specific attribution — becomes a durable reference point for every subsequent stage of the proceedings.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This explainer deals only with anticipatory bail for directors facing FIRs arising from commercial disputes. Offences under special statutes that contain express deeming provisions for persons in charge of a company follow a different liability analysis and are not covered here.</p></div>
